WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.135
-Seth, it is so nice to see you.

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.903
The audience loves you.

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.371
-It's great to be
in front of an audience.

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:07.206
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:10.543
-I watch your show all the time
and you don't have --

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.845
you haven't had an audience
since the pandemic.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:14.681
-A week from today,
they're coming back.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:16.382
-Wow.
-Yeah, it's very exciting.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:18.283
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Now, are you -- are --

00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:19.853
Is it going to throw you?

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.921
-Well, here's what I think.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:24.957
It'll be nice to write a joke,
and then if the audience laughs,

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:27.160
I will think I was right.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.061
And then it will also be nice
if I write a joke

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:30.129
and the audience doesn't laugh,

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:31.798
to be able to think
they were wrong.

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:33.266
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:34.601
-Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah, you know, that's --

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:35.902
That's, I think, where
I'm going to be at.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:37.804
-Yeah, I think the guests
will enjoy it as well.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.972
-I think the guests -- you know,
I got used to

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:41.174
not being in front of
an audience,

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.576
but I think the guests,
when they walk out,

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.245
they feel like
they've been scammed.

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:46.612
[ Laughter ]

00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:48.180
They walk out and they're like,
"Oh, no, I gave them

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.715
my Social Security number."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:51.651
-"There's no one here."
-"There is no one here."

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.919
-You're waving, doing nothing.
-You know what backfired for us?

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:55.388
-Unh-unh.
-You know Wally?

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:56.889
Cue card Wally.
-We love Wally.

00:00:56.889 --> 00:00:58.457
-Wally Feresten does
the cue cards for "SNL."

00:00:58.457 --> 00:00:59.458
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:01.161
-Yeah, there you go.

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:03.395
You know, he -- you know,
out of necessity,

00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:04.831
he's just been on camera a lot,

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.032
'cause we don't have
an audience.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:07.800
-Oh, no.
-Straight to his head, Jimmy.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.502
-Oh, you created a monster.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.271
-Half the time the cue cards
don't have the script.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:14.173
It just says,
"People want Wally."

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.108
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:18.077
-He's just giving out
his agent's number

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:19.779
so he can get booked for parties
and stuff.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.046
-He's always been a nightmare.

00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:22.748
-Yeah, well, I'll have
a talk with Wally.

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:24.984
-Thank you. If you would,
I would really appreciate it.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:27.153
-You know what I did see that
you did?

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.288
You brought back,
because now bars are open,

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.723
but your day-drinking segment.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:31.857
-Yeah, I went day-drinking
again.

00:01:31.857 --> 00:01:33.259
-You went day-drinking
with Lorde.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.761
-I did.
-It was very funny.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:37.831
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.265
How was that to get
back out there?

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.301
-It was good. I hate to
break celebrity gossip

00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:44.470
because that's never been
what I'm about,

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.273
but Lorde is younger than me.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.708
-Wow!
-Yeah.

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:50.143
-Is that right?
-Lorde is younger than me.

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:54.680
And when day drinking started,
I was acutely aware of that.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.282
-Mm-hmm.
-And then the more I drank,

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.952
the more I thought,
"We're just young people."

00:01:58.952 --> 00:01:59.952
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:02.154
"Lorde and I are
two young people."

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:04.624
-"We're the same age."
-"And we can drink what we want.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.560
[ Laughter ]
And it won't matter that I have

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.894
kids at home
who are gonna wake up

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:10.562
at the same time
they always wake up."

00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:11.764
-Yes, of course.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.666
Well, maybe they'll sleep in
out of nowhere.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.001
-I came home late and was like,

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:16.636
"You guys got to sleep
in tomorrow."

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.604
-They go, "No problem, Dad.
Yeah. Of course."

00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:20.106
How are old are they?
How old are the kids?

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.207
-3 1/2 and 5 1/2.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.308
-Yeah. Oh, my gosh.
[ Audience aws ]

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.309
-The best, exactly.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.510
-That's the cutest age, yeah.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.645
-It's so great that
it's just, like,

00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:27.881
that is why being a kid
is great.

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:30.850
Just to be those ages
gets an "aw."

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.618
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:35.321
If I'm like, "47,"
no one's like, "Aw."

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.756
-"Aw." Yeah, exactly.
[ Audience aws, laughs ]

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.158
Oh, you did get an "aw."
Actually, yeah, you sold it.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.127
You sold it very well.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.162
-I know that you -- we talked
about this the last time --

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:45.264
you announced you're writing
a children's book.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:46.399
-The cover came out today.
Look at that.

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:47.733
-Look at this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:49.334
-Look at that.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:50.670
-"I'm Not Scared,
You're Scared!"

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.171
-Yep.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:54.173
-I love the title.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.474
Was that the original title?

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:58.110
-Originally it was,
"I'm not drunk, you're drunk."

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.212
-That is not true.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:00.280
-No, it was.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.748
And then they said,
"It was funny."

00:03:02.748 --> 00:03:04.584
-Yeah, yeah.
That was a funny idea.

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.286
Did your -- Did you use
your kids as a sounding board?

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.688
-I did. I read it.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.989
Well, first, as I was
percolating the idea,

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:12.892
I would tell it to them
like a bedtime story,

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:15.594
about this bear who was scared
and a rabbit who wasn't.

00:03:15.594 --> 00:03:17.930
And I told it the first time.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.766
I was feeling really good
about the story I had.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.735
And my 5 1/2-year-old said,
"That part is too scary.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.036
One part is too scary."

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.072
And I was like, "Oh, that's
really good to hear."

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:28.374
And then the 3 1/2-year-old,
who echoes everything he said,

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.376
said, "Yeah, that part is
too scary."

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.378
And I was like, "You need to
have your own ideas."

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.646
[ Laughter ]
-"Yeah, you just heard him

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:34.814
say that."
-Yeah, I don't need it --

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:36.849
I don't need to hear it twice.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.718
I don't need the negative
feedback twice.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.086
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:43.322
He's got a Smithers to his
Mr. Burns, just being a yes-man.

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:44.991
-What are the kids going to be
for Halloween?

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.759
Have they decided?

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:49.361
-The 5-year-old wants to
be a sea captain

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:52.332
and the 3-year-old wants to be
a pirate, which is good.

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:54.467
It's thematically good.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.836
-Yeah.
-But then the 5-year-old keeps

00:03:56.836 --> 00:04:00.873
trying to make the 3-year-old's
costume more complicated.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.176
So the 3-year-old will be like,
"I want to be a pirate."

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:05.511
And he's like, "Do you want to
be a pirate or do you want to be

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:07.780
a pirate on a ship
so you can have a boat?"

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.282
And I'm like,
"Don't up-sell him."

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:12.018
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, he's happy.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:13.986
-Yeah, he's happy.

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.321
Like, "You're not going to
build the boat. Cut it out."

00:04:16.321 --> 00:04:17.390
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.225
We're putting an eye patch --
-Yeah.

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:21.794
It's like a guy at an Audi
dealership being like,

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:24.163
"We can get you snow tires."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:26.265
-"Hey, butt out, buddy."
-"He wants the car."

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.467
-Yeah. But aren't you --
aren't you guys --

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.203
Are you boat or beach people?
Aren't you a family?

00:04:31.203 --> 00:04:33.272
-My wife and her family
are beach people.

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:34.574
-Yes.
-I'm not a beach person at all.

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:36.742
I dislike the beach greatly.

00:04:36.742 --> 00:04:40.412
And my kids love it, which is
a bummer, because I go --

00:04:40.412 --> 00:04:41.881
I would do anything
my kids love.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:42.915
-Of course.
-You know?

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.917
But at the same time,
if something

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.053
traumatic would happen
that would make them not go

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:49.154
or not want to go,
I'd be all for that.

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:50.590
And --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.291
My 3 1/2-year-old has a bagel --

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.625
This is a true story
that happened this summer.

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:54.827
I wasn't there to see it.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.696
I'm so deeply bummed that
I wasn't there to see it.

00:04:56.696 --> 00:04:58.297
[ Laughter ]
'Cause he was walking with

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:00.999
a bagel, and from what I hear,
he was,

00:05:00.999 --> 00:05:03.469
to put it in nautical terms,
"asking for it."

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.938
[ Laughter ]
He was holding a bagel up

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:09.308
like this and a seagull
came down and, like --

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:12.077
like it was a falconry show
at a ren fair.

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:14.146
[ Laughter ]
Just, like, came down

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:16.382
and grabbed the bagel,
and I guess he just, like,

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.951
burst into tears, as you would
if you're a 3 1/2-year-old.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.686
I mean, you're walking down the
bea-- I mean, it did seem like

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.321
he was, maybe,
being a little show-offy like,

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:23.656
"I got a bagel."
-"I got a bagel."

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.625
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I know that.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:28.827
-But the best part about it,
is now,

00:05:28.827 --> 00:05:31.597
he thinks there's
just one seagull.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.032
Like, he doesn't realize
there's different sea--

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:36.202
So every seagull he sees, he's
like, "Don't take my bagel!"

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.803
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:39.372
-See? That's how you --
-Yeah.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.573
-So one kid solved.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:41.573 --> 00:05:43.976
-Yeah. Seth, I'm always happy
when you come on the show.

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:45.177
You're doing such a great job
with the show, buddy.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:46.178
-Always a pleasure.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:47.680
-Congrats.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.548
"I'm Not Scared, You're Scared!"

00:05:49.548 --> 00:05:52.518
Check this out, everybody.
Seth Meyers.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:54.954
Check out his show,
"Late Night with Seth Meyers,"

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:56.589
after us, tonight!

